# measurement-mass-by-object-type.rq — PROJ-766, Rail G measurement profile
# (PRD.md sec.16/22). Computes the raw per-family execution measure mu_x at
# the "object-centric aggregation level" declared process scale: mass_i =
# count of admitted G_OCEL events attributed to object-type family i,
# grouped by ocel:objectTypeName reached via ocel:relatesTo ->
# ocel:hasObjectType (otel-to-ocel.construct.rq, PROJ-764, already asserts
# this on every derived ocel:Event: the required process.object.type OTLP
# attribute becomes ocel:Object -> ocel:hasObjectType -> ocel:ObjectType ->
# ocel:objectTypeName). Unlike the other two real scales (workflow,
# activity), this one indirects through the related ocel:Object node rather
# than reading a same-subject property -- that indirection mirrors OCEL
# 2.0's own object-centric design: the "aggregation level" this scale
# measures is exactly OCEL's core organizing dimension, object type. Raw
# count only; see measurement-mass-by-workflow.rq's header for the
# normalization/statistics scope boundary, which applies identically here.
#
# Compiled into crates/cng/src/measurement.rs via include_str! (library
# query, per the crate's no-inline-SPARQL convention).
PREFIX ocel: <https://www.ocel-standard.org/2.0/>

SELECT ?family (COUNT(?ev) AS ?mass)
WHERE {
  GRAPH <urn:graph:ocel> {
    ?ev ocel:relatesTo ?obj .
    ?obj ocel:hasObjectType ?otype .
    ?otype ocel:objectTypeName ?family .
  }
}
GROUP BY ?family
ORDER BY ?family
